I left out the enrichment fetch. The node's `with` clause grants only `aap:caps/state-rw@0.1.0`, and the spec makes that clause the node's entire authority. An `http-client` import would give the node authority the graph does not declare. The request would also send each input's raw text to an external host, which is a data egress the capability model exists to gate. I can add it once the graph's `with` clause lists `http-client` with the host allowlist, and once we decide how the input text is encoded and minimized.

The node below records each considered id under the `seen` store, using the digest's date as the value. It records only when the digest was published, so items from an unpublished digest stay eligible for the next run. Its output is the same as it would be without the fetch.

```wit
package gen:node;

world node {
    use aap:caps/types@0.1.0.{published-digest, run-report};

    import aap:caps/state-rw@0.1.0;

    export run: func(published: published-digest) -> run-report;
}
```

```rust
wit_bindgen::generate!({
    path: "wit",
    world: "node",
    generate_all,
});

use crate::aap::caps::state_rw;

struct Node;

impl Guest for Node {
    fn run(published: PublishedDigest) -> RunReport {
        let mut recorded: u32 = 0;

        if published.published {
            for id in &published.considered {
                state_rw::put(id, &published.date);
                recorded = recorded.saturating_add(1);
            }
        }

        RunReport {
            date: published.date,
            recorded,
        }
    }
}

export!(Node);
```

